Invertibility gives $ell(e(x)) = x$ and $e(ell(y)) = y$. Writing $y = e(u)$ and $z = e(v)$,

$
  ell(y z) = ell(e(u) e(v)) = ell(e(u+v)) = u + v = ell(y) + ell(z).
$

The Inverse Function Theorem gives $ell'(y) = 1 \/ e'(ell(y)) = 1 \/ y$, so since $y > 0$ on its domain, $ell$ is monotone. Finally $ell(1) = 0$ because $e(0) = 1$, and the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus turns $ell' = 1\/y$ into

$
  ell(y) = integral_1^y ell'(t) dif t = integral_1^y dif t \/ t.
$
